                    Pino logger for Node.js
                </h1>
                <p class="mt-7 max-w-[54ch] font-display text-[17px] leading-[1.6] text-fg-muted md:text-lg">
                    Set up Pino, configure it for production, log HTTP requests, and tie every line to the trace it came from.
                </p>
                <div class="mt-7 flex flex-wrap items-center gap-x-5 gap-y-2 font-mono text-[10px] text-fg-muted">
                    <a href={authorUrl} rel="noopener" class="flex items-center gap-2 transition-colors hover:text-fg">
                        <img src={author.avatar} alt="" width="20" height="20" class="h-5 w-5 rounded-full ring-1 ring-border" />
                        <span>By {author.name}</span>
                    </a>
                    <time datetime="2026-10-20">Updated {updatedLabel}</time>
                </div>
            </div>
        </header>

        <nav aria-label="Guide sections" class="border-b border-border bg-bg-deep">
            <div class="mx-auto flex max-w-6xl gap-6 overflow-x-auto px-6 py-4 font-mono text-[10px] whitespace-nowrap text-fg-muted">
                {sectionNav.map((h) => (
                    <a href={`#${h.slug}`} class="transition-colors hover:text-primary">{h.text}</a>
                ))}
            </div>
        </nav>

        <div class="mx-auto grid max-w-6xl gap-12 px-6 py-16 md:py-24 lg:grid-cols-[14rem_minmax(0,1fr)] lg:gap-16">
            <aside class="hidden lg:block">
                <TableOfContents headings={toc} class="sticky top-28" />
            </aside>

            <article class="pino-guide min-w-0">
                <section class="guide-lead">
                    <p>
                        <strong>Pino</strong> is a structured logger for Node.js. It writes every log entry as one line of JSON with a level, a timestamp and any fields you pass, and it is designed to add as little overhead as possible to your application.
                    </p>
                    <p>
                        This guide covers installing Pino and writing your first logs, configuring it for production, logging HTTP requests with pino-http or Fastify, adding trace IDs with OpenTelemetry, and how Pino compares with Winston.
                    </p>

                    <div class="guide-takeaways">
                        <p class="guide-kicker">Key takeaways</p>
                        <ul>
                            <li>Pass data as an object first: <code>logger.info(&#123; orderId &#125;, "order created")</code> gives you a field to search.</li>
                            <li>A child logger adds a request ID or tenant to every line it writes.</li>
                            <li>List secret paths in <code>redact</code>; a token under an unlisted key still goes out in clear text.</li>
                            <li>Use <code>pino-pretty</code> in development only and keep production output as JSON.</li>
                            <li>With the OpenTelemetry Pino instrumentation, each line carries the trace ID of the request that wrote it.</li>
                        </ul>
                    </div>
                </section>

                <section id="what-is-pino" class="scroll-mt-24">
                    <p class="guide-eyebrow"><span>01</span> · Definition</p>
                    <h2>Pino, the JSON logger for Node.js</h2>

                    <p>
                        JSON lines suit services that run in containers or on platforms that collect stdout: whatever reads the output can filter on fields instead of matching text. Pino is MIT-licensed, Fastify uses it as its built-in logger, and <code>pino-http</code> adds request logging to Express and plain <code>http</code> servers.
                    </p>
                    <p>
                        Every call takes the same path. On the thread that runs your code, Pino drops calls below the configured level and turns the rest into a JSON line. Where the line goes next depends on your setup:
                    </p>

                    <figure class="flow" aria-label="The path of a Pino log line. Your code calls logger.info. On the main thread, Pino checks the level and writes one JSON line. By default the line goes straight to a destination such as stdout or a file. With pino.transport it goes to a worker thread, which formats it and writes it to a terminal or file. With the OpenTelemetry pino instrumentation, the same line also goes to the OpenTelemetry log SDK, which batches records and exports them to Maple at /v1/logs.">
                        <svg viewBox="0 0 760 290" aria-hidden="true">
                            <defs>
                                {Object.entries(stages).map(([key, s]) => (
                                    <marker id={`flow-arrow-${key}`} viewBox="0 0 8 8" refX="7" refY="4" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto-start-reverse">
                                        <path d="M0,0 L8,4 L0,8 z" style={`fill: ${s.color}`} />
                                    </marker>
                                ))}
                            </defs>

                            <rect x="0" y="105" width="140" height="80" rx="8" class="flow__box" />
                            <text x="70" y="139" text-anchor="middle" class="flow__title">Your code</text>
                            <text x="70" y="161" text-anchor="middle" class="flow__mono">logger.info()</text>
                            <line x1="140" y1="145" x2="180" y2="145" marker-end="url(#flow-arrow-main)" class="flow__line" style={`--stage: ${stages.main.color}`} />

                            <rect x="186" y="95" width="140" height="100" rx="8" class="flow__box flow__box--stage" style={`--stage: ${stages.main.color}`} />
                            <text x="256" y="124" text-anchor="middle" class="flow__title">Pino</text>
                            <text x="256" y="143" text-anchor="middle" class="flow__mono flow__mono--stage" style={`--stage: ${stages.main.color}`}>main thread</text>
                            <text x="256" y="165" text-anchor="middle" class="flow__mono">level check</text>
                            <text x="256" y="181" text-anchor="middle" class="flow__mono">-&gt; one JSON line</text>

                            <line x1="332" y1="112" x2="470" y2="48" marker-end="url(#flow-arrow-destination)" class="flow__line" style={`--stage: ${stages.destination.color}`} />
                            <text x="382" y="68" text-anchor="middle" class="flow__mono">default</text>
                            <rect x="476" y="18" width="140" height="58" rx="8" class="flow__box flow__box--stage" style={`--stage: ${stages.destination.color}`} />
                            <text x="546" y="43" text-anchor="middle" class="flow__title">Destination</text>
                            <text x="546" y="63" text-anchor="middle" class="flow__mono">stdout or a file</text>

                            <line x1="332" y1="145" x2="470" y2="145" marker-end="url(#flow-arrow-worker)" class="flow__line" style={`--stage: ${stages.worker.color}`} />
                            <text x="401" y="137" text-anchor="middle" class="flow__mono">or pino.transport()</text>
                            <rect x="476" y="116" width="140" height="58" rx="8" class="flow__box flow__box--stage" style={`--stage: ${stages.worker.color}`} />
                            <text x="546" y="141" text-anchor="middle" class="flow__title">Transport</text>
                            <text x="546" y="161" text-anchor="middle" class="flow__mono flow__mono--stage" style={`--stage: ${stages.worker.color}`}>worker thread</text>
                            <line x1="616" y1="145" x2="650" y2="145" marker-end="url(#flow-arrow-destination)" class="flow__line" style={`--stage: ${stages.destination.color}`} />
                            <rect x="656" y="116" width="104" height="58" rx="8" class="flow__box flow__box--stage" style={`--stage: ${stages.destination.color}`} />
                            <text x="708" y="141" text-anchor="middle" class="flow__title">Destination</text>
                            <text x="708" y="161" text-anchor="middle" class="flow__mono">pretty, file</text>

                            <line x1="332" y1="178" x2="470" y2="244" marker-end="url(#flow-arrow-otel)" class="flow__line flow__line--dashed" style={`--stage: ${stages.otel.color}`} />
                            <text x="372" y="236" text-anchor="middle" class="flow__mono">and, with</text>
                            <text x="372" y="251" text-anchor="middle" class="flow__mono">OpenTelemetry</text>
                            <rect x="476" y="216" width="140" height="58" rx="8" class="flow__box flow__box--stage" style={`--stage: ${stages.otel.color}`} />
                            <text x="546" y="241" text-anchor="middle" class="flow__title">OTel log SDK</text>
                            <text x="546" y="261" text-anchor="middle" class="flow__mono">batches records</text>
                            <line x1="616" y1="245" x2="650" y2="245" marker-end="url(#flow-arrow-otel)" class="flow__line" style={`--stage: ${stages.otel.color}`} />
                            <rect x="656" y="216" width="104" height="58" rx="8" class="flow__box flow__box--filled" style={`--stage: ${stages.otel.color}`} />
                            <text x="708" y="241" text-anchor="middle" class="flow__title">Maple</text>
                            <text x="708" y="261" text-anchor="middle" class="flow__mono">/v1/logs</text>
                        </svg>
                    </figure>
                    <p class="guide-caption">A destination and a transport are alternatives. The OpenTelemetry path is added on top of either one, so the line still reaches stdout or your transport.</p>
                </section>

                <section id="setup" class="scroll-mt-24">
                    <p class="guide-eyebrow"><span>02</span> · Setup</p>
                    <h2>Install Pino and write the first logs</h2>

                    <h3 id="first-log" class="scroll-mt-24">The first log line</h3>
                    <p>Pino is one package and needs no configuration to start:</p>
                    <InstallTabs command="npm install pino" />
                    <LanguageTabs label="Module format" tabs={firstLogTabs.map(({ id, label }) => ({ id, label }))}>
                        {firstLogTabs.map((t) => (
                            <LanguageTab id={t.id}>
                                <p class="client-install"><span>Run</span><code>{t.run}</code></p>
                                <pre><code set:html={highlight(t.code, "javascript")} /></pre>
                            </LanguageTab>
                        ))}
                    </LanguageTabs>
                    <div class="code-file code-file--output"><p class="code-file__name">Output</p><pre><code set:html={highlight(firstLogOutput, "json")} /></pre></div>
                    <p>
                        Each method takes an optional object first and the message second. The object's keys become top-level fields of the line, which is what lets you filter on <code>port</code> later. Arguments after the message fill <code>%s</code>, <code>%d</code> and <code>%o</code> placeholders, and an <code>Error</code> passed first is written under <code>err</code> with its type, message and stack.
                    </p>
                    <p>
                        Put data in the object, not in the message. <code>logger.info(&#123; orderId &#125;, "order created")</code> gives you an <code>orderId</code> field to search; a template string gives you text to parse back.
                    </p>

                    <h3 id="log-levels" class="scroll-mt-24">Pino log levels</h3>
                    <p>
                        Pino writes the level as a number. A call below the logger's level returns before any serialization, so a disabled <code>debug</code> call costs almost nothing.
                    </p>
                    <div class="guide-table">
                        <table>
                            <thead>
                                <tr><th>Level</th><th>Value</th><th>Use it for</th></tr>
                            </thead>
                            <tbody>
                                {levels.map((l) => (
                                    <tr>
                                        <td><span class="level-badge" style={`--tone: ${l.tone}`}>{l.name}</span></td>
                                        <td class="mono">{l.value}</td>
                                        <td>{l.use}</td>
                                    </tr>
                                ))}
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                    </div>
                    <p>
                        The default is <code>info</code>, and <code>silent</code> turns logging off, for example in tests. Read the level from an environment variable so one deploy can run at <code>debug</code> without a code change:
                    </p>
                    <pre><code set:html={highlight(levelSnippet, "javascript")} /></pre>
                    <p>
                        A disabled call still evaluates its arguments, so wrap expensive ones in <code>isLevelEnabled()</code>. The <code>customLevels</code> option adds levels with your own names and numbers.
                    </p>

                    <h3 id="child-loggers" class="scroll-mt-24">Child loggers</h3>
                    <p>
                        <code>logger.child(bindings)</code> returns a logger that adds the same fields to every line it writes. Pino serializes the bindings once, when the child is created, so a child per request is cheap.
                    </p>
                    <pre><code set:html={highlight(childSnippet, "javascript")} /></pre>
                    <div class="code-file code-file--output"><p class="code-file__name">Output</p><pre><code set:html={highlight(childOutput, "json")} /></pre></div>
                    <p>
                        The usual pattern is one child per request or job, carrying a request ID or tenant, passed to the code that handles it. The second argument to <code>child()</code> overrides <code>level</code>, <code>redact</code>, <code>serializers</code> or <code>msgPrefix</code> for that child only.
                    </p>
                </section>

                <section id="production" class="scroll-mt-24">
                    <p class="guide-eyebrow"><span>03</span> · Production</p>
                    <h2>Configure Pino for production</h2>

                    <h3 id="redaction" class="scroll-mt-24">Redact secrets and trim objects</h3>
                    <p>
                        <code>redact</code> replaces values at the paths you list before the line is written. Paths use dot and bracket notation, <code>[*]</code> matches every array element, and keys with hyphens need brackets. A <strong>serializer</strong> is a function keyed by a top-level field name: when a line has that field, Pino writes the function's return value instead of the object.
                    </p>
                    <pre><code set:html={highlight(redactSnippet, "javascript")} /></pre>
                    <div class="code-file code-file--output"><p class="code-file__name">Output</p><pre><code set:html={highlight(redactOutput, "json")} /></pre></div>
                    <p>
                        The default censor is <code>"[Redacted]"</code>; <code>remove: true</code> drops the key instead. Redaction only covers the paths you list, so a token under a new key name goes out in clear text. Logging whole request bodies is the larger risk, and a serializer that picks a few fields avoids it. <code>pino.stdSerializers</code> provides <code>err</code>, <code>req</code> and <code>res</code>; <code>err</code> is on by default.
                    </p>

                    <h3 id="pino-pretty" class="scroll-mt-24">pino-pretty in development only</h3>
                    <p>
                        <code>pino-pretty</code> turns JSON lines into colored, readable ones. Install it as a dev dependency and load it as a transport only when <code>NODE_ENV</code> is <code>development</code>, so a production install without dev dependencies never asks for it:
                    </p>
                    <InstallTabs command="npm install --save-dev pino-pretty" />
                    <pre><code set:html={highlight(prettySnippet, "javascript")} /></pre>
                    <div class="code-file code-file--output"><p class="code-file__name">Output</p><pre><code set:html={prettyOutput} /></pre></div>
                    <p>Or leave the code alone and pipe the output through the CLI, which takes the same options as flags:</p>
                    <InstallTabs variants={prettyCli} />
                    <p>
                        The pino-pretty README advises against running it in production: keep production output as JSON so the tool that collects it can read the fields.
                    </p>

                    <h3 id="transports" class="scroll-mt-24"><span class="stage-dot" style={`--stage: ${stages.worker.color}`}></span>Transports run in a worker thread</h3>
                    <p>
                        A <strong>transport</strong> is a module that receives Pino's lines and writes them somewhere. <code>pino.transport()</code> starts it in a worker thread, so formatting and network I/O don't compete with request handling. With <code>targets</code>, one logger feeds several transports, each with its own minimum level:
                    </p>
                    <pre><code set:html={highlight(transportSnippet, "javascript")} /></pre>
                    <ul class="guide-list">
                        <li><code>target</code> is a package name or an absolute path to your own module. <code>pino/file</code> ships with Pino.</li>
                        <li>A target without a <code>level</code> gets <code>info</code>. The logger's level applies first, so set it to the lowest level any target needs: <code>debug</code> in the example.</li>
                        <li><code>options</code> is copied to the worker, so it cannot contain functions.</li>
                        <li>Pino flushes the worker on a normal exit. The Pino docs treat an <code>'error'</code> event from a transport as fatal.</li>
                    </ul>

                    <h3 id="destinations" class="scroll-mt-24">Sync, async and buffered writes</h3>
                    <p>
                        Without a transport, Pino writes on the main thread through a destination built on <code>sonic-boom</code>. <code>pino.destination()</code> changes where it writes and when:
                    </p>
                    <pre><code set:html={highlight(destinationSnippet, "javascript")} /></pre>
                    <div class="guide-table">
                        <table>
                            <thead>
                                <tr><th>Setting</th><th>Runs on</th><th>Behaviour</th><th>Use it when</th></tr>
                            </thead>
                            <tbody>
                                {writeModes.map((w) => (
                                    <tr>
                                        <td>{w.setting.includes(":") || w.setting.includes("(") ? <code class="nowrap">{w.setting}</code> : w.setting}</td>
                                        <td><span class="stage-badge" style={`--stage: ${w.runs.color}`}>{w.runs.name}</span></td>
                                        <td>{w.behaviour}</td>
                                        <td>{w.when}</td>
                                    </tr>
                                ))}
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                    </div>
                    <p>
                        Pino's docs note that buffered logging in Lambda tends to delay or lose lines, because the runtime freezes the process before the buffer is written.
                    </p>
                </section>

                <section id="http" class="scroll-mt-24">
                    <p class="guide-eyebrow"><span>04</span> · HTTP</p>
                    <h2>Log HTTP requests with pino-http or Fastify</h2>
                    <p>
                        A request log needs one line per request with its status and duration, plus a per-request child logger so every line a handler writes carries the same request ID. Express gets both from <code>pino-http</code>; Fastify has them built in.
                    </p>
                    <LanguageTabs label="Framework" tabs={httpTabs.map(({ id, label }) => ({ id, label }))}>
                        {httpTabs.map((t) => (
                            <LanguageTab id={t.id}>
                                <InstallTabs command={t.install} />
                                <pre><code set:html={highlight(t.code, "javascript")} /></pre>
                                <p class="tab-note" set:html={t.note} />
                            </LanguageTab>
                        ))}
                    </LanguageTabs>
                    <p>
                        Both servers write the handler's line with the request ID and the <code>orderId</code> field. The next section adds the trace ID to that same line.
                    </p>
                </section>

                <section id="opentelemetry" class="scroll-mt-24">
                    <p class="guide-eyebrow"><span>05</span> · Correlation</p>
                    <h2>Trace IDs and Pino logs in Maple</h2>
                    <p>
                        A line that says <code>payment provider unreachable</code> is more useful when you can open the request it came from. The OpenTelemetry pino instrumentation does two things, with no change to your logging calls:
                    </p>
                    <ul class="guide-list">
                        <li>Lines written inside an active span get <span class="trace-field">trace_id</span>, <span class="trace-field">span_id</span> and <span class="trace-field">trace_flags</span> fields.</li>
                        <li>Pino's output is split in two: one copy goes to your destination or transport as before, the other to the OpenTelemetry log SDK, which batches the records and exports them over OTLP. This needs Pino 7 or later.</li>
                    </ul>

                    <h3 id="otel-setup" class="scroll-mt-24"><span class="stage-dot" style={`--stage: ${stages.otel.color}`}></span>Start the OpenTelemetry SDK</h3>
                    <p>
                        The instrumentation is part of <code>@opentelemetry/auto-instrumentations-node</code>, so the standard Node.js SDK setup turns it on. Install the SDK packages:
                    </p>
                    <InstallTabs command={otelInstall} />
                    <p>
                        Create <code>tracing.mjs</code> next to <code>server.mjs</code>. It starts the SDK before your code loads Pino, which is what lets the instrumentation patch it. <code>MAPLE_KEY</code> is an ingest key from Maple, covered in the <a href="#maple">Maple steps</a> below.
                    </p>
                    <pre><code set:html={highlight(tracingSnippet, "javascript")} /></pre>
                    <div class="guide-callout">
                        <p class="guide-kicker">Check the BatchLogRecordProcessor signature</p>
                        <p>
                            Current <code>@opentelemetry/sdk-logs</code> releases take an options object, <code>new BatchLogRecordProcessor(&#123; exporter &#125;)</code>. Older releases took the exporter as the first argument. Passing an exporter to a release that expects the object raises no error, and no log record leaves the process, so check the installed <code>.d.ts</code> if logs never arrive.
                        </p>
                    </div>
                    <p>
                        An ingest key can only write telemetry to your organization; it cannot read data. To keep it out of source anyway, the SDK can take its whole configuration from the standard <code>OTEL_*</code> variables, as in <a href="/docs/guides/instrumentation-nodejs#environment-variables">the Node.js guide's environment variables section</a>.
                    </p>

                    <h3 id="correlated" class="scroll-mt-24">The same line, with a trace ID</h3>
                    <p>Load <code>tracing.mjs</code> ahead of either server from the HTTP section:</p>
                    <pre><code set:html={highlight("node --import ./tracing.mjs server.mjs", "shell")} /></pre>
                    <div class="code-file code-file--output"><p class="code-file__name">Output</p><pre><code set:html={highlight(correlatedOutput, "json")} /></pre></div>
                    <p>
                        Stdout now carries the trace context. The exported copy uses Pino's level name as the severity text, with the matching OpenTelemetry severity number, and your fields, such as <code>orderId</code> and <code>reqId</code>, become log attributes. It leaves out <code>pid</code>, <code>hostname</code> and the trace fields, which the resource and the record's trace context already carry. Lines written outside a span, such as the startup <code>listening</code> line, are exported without a trace ID.
                    </p>
                    <p>
                        The export receives every line the logger writes, whatever the per-target levels of a <code>pino.transport()</code>. To keep the trace IDs and ship logs another way, turn log sending off in <code>tracing.mjs</code>:
                    </p>
                    <pre><code set:html={highlight(disableSendingSnippet, "javascript")} /></pre>

                    <h3 id="maple" class="scroll-mt-24">Find the logs in Maple</h3>
                    <ol class="guide-steps">
                        <li>
                            <span>01</span>
                            <div><h4>Create an ingest key</h4><p>Open <strong>Settings → Ingestion</strong> and copy the private key (<code>maple_sk_...</code>). Use it as <code>MAPLE_KEY</code> in <code>tracing.mjs</code>, and switch <code>MAPLE_ENDPOINT</code> to the EU host if your organization is in the EU region.</p></div>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <span>02</span>
                            <div><h4>Filter the Logs page</h4><p>Start the server with the SDK and send it a few requests. In <strong>Logs</strong>, filter <strong>Service</strong> to <code>orders-api</code>. The <strong>Severity</strong> filter lists Pino's level names. Paste a trace ID into the search box to see every line from one request.</p>
                                <figure class="guide-figure"><img src="/screenshots/guides/pino-logger-01-logs.webp" data-zoomable alt="The Logs page filtered to the orders-api service, listing Pino's loading order and request completed lines with their time, level and attributes, next to the severity and service filters." width="2362" height="1330" loading="lazy" /></figure></div>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <span>03</span>
                            <div><h4>Open a line</h4><p>Click a <code>loading order</code> row, then <strong>Open detail</strong>. <strong>Attributes</strong> shows <code>orderId</code> and <code>reqId</code>, and the meta strip shows the <code>trace:</code> link and span ID.</p>
                                <figure class="guide-figure guide-figure--narrow"><img src="/screenshots/guides/pino-logger-02-log-detail.webp" data-zoomable alt="The detail sheet of a loading order log line: the message, a meta strip with the timestamp, trace link and span ID, and log attributes orderId 42 and reqId." width="1342" height="690" loading="lazy" /></figure></div>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <span>04</span>
                            <div><h4>Move to the trace</h4><p>Click the <code>trace:</code> link to open the request's spans at the log's timestamp. From a trace, <strong>View Logs</strong> goes the other way.</p>
                                <figure class="guide-figure"><img src="/screenshots/guides/pino-logger-03-trace.webp" data-zoomable alt="The trace of a GET /orders/:id request in orders-api, opened from the log line's trace link: the HTTP server span with the Express middleware and request handler spans below it." width="2334" height="1022" loading="lazy" /></figure></div>
                        </li>
                    </ol>

                    <div class="guide-maple">
                        <p class="guide-kicker">Set it up in Maple</p>
                        <h3>Send your Node.js telemetry</h3>
                        <p>The SDK setup, environment variables and troubleshooting are in the docs.</p>
                        <div class="mt-6 flex flex-wrap gap-3">
                            <a href="/docs/guides/instrumentation-nodejs#log-correlation" class="guide-button guide-button--primary">Node.js instrumentation</a>
                            <a href="/docs/explore/logs" class="guide-button">Logs in Maple</a>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                </section>

                <section id="pino-vs-winston" class="scroll-mt-24">
                    <p class="guide-eyebrow"><span>06</span> · Comparison</p>
                    <h2>Pino vs Winston</h2>
                    <p>
                        Winston is the other widely used Node.js logger. Both are MIT-licensed and both work with OpenTelemetry. They differ mostly in where formatting and shipping happen.
                    </p>
                    <div class="guide-table">
                        <table>
                            <thead>
                                <tr><th></th><th>Pino</th><th>Winston</th></tr>
                            </thead>
                            <tbody>
                                {winston.map(([row, pino, win]) => (
                                    <tr><td><strong>{row}</strong></td><td set:html={pino} /><td set:html={win} /></tr>
                                ))}
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                    </div>
                    <p>
                        In <a href="https://github.com/pinojs/pino/blob/main/docs/benchmarks.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Pino's own benchmark</a>, a run of <code>logger.info('hello world')</code> calls averaged 114.8 ms with Pino and 270.2 ms with Winston.
                    </p>
                    <p>
                        Choose Pino when logs are JSON headed for a collector and per-call cost matters. Choose Winston when the process itself must write several formats to several destinations, or an existing codebase depends on its format pipeline. Moving between them is mostly mechanical: both take a metadata object and a message, and Pino puts the object first.
                    </p>
                </section>

                <section class="guide-sources">
                    <p class="guide-eyebrow">Sources and further reading</p>
                    <ul>
                        <li><a href="https://getpino.io/#/docs/api" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Pino: API reference</a></li>
                        <li><a href="https://getpino.io/#/docs/transports" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Pino: Transports</a></li>
                        <li><a href="https://getpino.io/#/docs/redaction" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Pino: Redaction</a></li>
                        <li><a href="https://getpino.io/#/docs/asynchronous" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Pino: Asynchronous logging</a></li>
                        <li><a href="https://github.com/pinojs/pino-pretty" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">pino-pretty README</a></li>
                        <li><a href="https://github.com/pinojs/pino-http" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">pino-http README</a></li>
                        <li><a href="https://fastify.dev/docs/latest/Reference/Logging/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Fastify: Logging</a></li>
                        <li><a href="https://github.com/winstonjs/winston" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Winston README</a></li>
                        <li><a href="https://www.npmjs.com/package/@opentelemetry/instrumentation-pino" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">OpenTelemetry: instrumentation-pino</a></li>
                        <li><a href="/docs/guides/instrumentation-nodejs">Maple docs: Node.js instrumentation</a></li>
                        <li><a href="/docs/explore/logs">Maple docs: Logs</a></li>
                    </ul>
                </section>
            </article>
        </div>
    </main>

    <FAQ items={faqs} />
